topics.body:
Build each shootout arm's prebuilt Levanter rows from canonical generation traces:
- plain: GLM's first passing trajectory per eligible task;
- RS-multi: per epoch, one passing attempt per plain task slot, without replacement;
- relay: passing relay episodes with a GLM turn, subsampled to plain's B;
- validation: one shared set, the first passing trajectory per held-out task.

The loss covers GLM's turns only. v0's turns in relay episodes stay as context.

A trajectory is dropped when it was summarized, failed or raised, or does not fit 32,768 tokens. It is also dropped when it leaks:
- it holds a live proxy capability token (decoded JWT, audience iris-proxy);
- it reads mini-swe-agent's own tool environment;
- it reads a library copy of the task's repo.

Every arm gets the same replay rows: a seeded prefix of the replay slice, cut at R = round(B / 4) and checked by hash against plain's manifest.

Each arm writes three epoch files. Each is its own seeded permutation, padded with zero-loss rows to a multiple of 16, so a trainer that reads files in order with mixture block 16 sees every row exactly once per epoch. Levanter's default 2,048-row mixture block permutes across the epoch boundary.

The manifest records:
- per-epoch row counts, steps and rows_sha256;
- per-family supervised and context tokens;
- the builder commit and source hashes;
- each input trace file's sha256 and Harbor commit;
- the token gate over the exact inputs;
- the pool's held-out ids.

A production prefix gets _SUCCESS last.

On pool v5, the production plain arm has B = 28,933,580 supervised tokens: 13,594,868 SWE-smith and 15,338,712 TMax. Each epoch is 4,212 rows plus 12 padding rows, or 264 steps at batch 16. RS-multi's three epochs are within 0.07%, 0.07% and 0.64% of B. Both prefixes passed an independent re-read: no held-out task rows, the same replay rows as plain, and plain's epochs the same rows in different orders. They were built at commit 6f74d672ac; this branch's refactor writes byte-identical rows.

Stacked on #10252, which adds the sweep_data modules this builder imports.

Part of #10213

